      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/089281519cd8926eb187c67d465666838b8143b4dd86e081ef3c151613fd27fd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(4).$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(4),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[3,5].$ Notice that $x=4$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/68daf42f2d14b0820bd36db73a8abe7b4d3d449c2b5b865b1d2affd70e069b43.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (3,5)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-3-0}{5-3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$",
